      "question": "If upstream and downstream RCCBs have the same IΔn and no delay, is selectivity guaranteed?",
      "answer": "No. Equal IΔn with no intentional time delay does **not** guarantee selectivity between upstream and downstream RCCBs.\n\nFor coordinated selectivity, a common design rule is:\n\n- Upstream IΔn should be **at least 2×** the downstream IΔn.\n- The upstream device’s minimum no-trip time should be **longer** than the downstream device’s maximum trip time.\n- A **delayed or selective** upstream device is normally used.\n\nThe exact ratio and settings should come from the manufacturer’s coordination table, since selectivity is a system design property, not a given just because the two devices share the same sensitivity.\n\nSources:\n- Reference Abb Rcd Application\n- Reference Rccb Selectivity Coordination\n- Reference Abb S200 Catalogue\n- Reference Eaton Pln6 Catalogue",
